{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"books-catch-up-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Catch up on overdue bookkeeping","description":"Brings months of neglected books up to date in five checked steps - scope and deadlines, gathering records, coding from the bank, reconciling, and a handover pack for the accountant.","category":"accounting","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan","build","verify","ship"],"role":["founder","individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","dataset","document"],"output":["checklist","table","plan","questions"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["financial"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","tags":["backlog","bank-reconciliation","transaction-coding","tax-deadlines"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["reconcile-bank-account","set-up-simple-bookkeeping","prepare-for-accountant-meeting","separate-personal-and-business-spending"],"personas":["bookkeeper"]},"args":[{"name":"situation","description":"The business, its legal form, how far behind the books are (from which date), which accounts and cards exist, what software or spreadsheet you use, and any tax deadlines or letters you know about.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"country","description":"Country and region, because filing deadlines, penalties and record rules differ.","type":"string"}],"steps":[{"id":"scope","file":"steps/01-scope.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"books-catch-up/01-scope.md"},{"id":"gather","file":"steps/02-gather.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"books-catch-up/02-gather.md"},{"id":"code","file":"steps/03-code.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"books-catch-up/03-code.md"},{"id":"reconcile","file":"steps/04-reconcile.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","artifact":"books-catch-up/04-reconcile.md"},{"id":"handover","file":"steps/05-handover.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","artifact":"books-catch-up/05-handover.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Catches up on overdue books the way an experienced bookkeeper would: find the deadlines first, work from bank and card statements (which are complete even when receipts are not), code in monthly blocks, reconcile each month before moving on, and hand the accountant a clean pack with an honest list of gaps. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval; later steps reuse approved figures.\n\n<situation>\n{{situation}}\n</situation>\n{{#country}}\n\nCountry: {{country}}\n{{/country}}\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nRules for every step:\n- Deadlines before tidiness: if a tax return, sales-tax return or payroll filing is overdue or close, it sets the order of work.\n- The bank is the backbone. Every statement line gets coded; missing receipts are listed, never invented.\n- Use only figures the person gave; mark gaps [X]. Show arithmetic.\n- Tax treatment, penalties and late-filing options are questions for an accountant or the tax authority, never decided here.\n- If the person mentions tax authority letters, penalties or enforcement, say to contact the tax authority or an accountant now, because acting early usually limits penalties.\n- Calm, no blame: backlogs are common and fixable.","steps":[{"id":"scope","file":"steps/01-scope.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","artifact":"books-catch-up/01-scope.md","text":"# Step 1: Scope and deadlines\n\n1. Confirm the period to catch up, the legal form, every bank, card, loan and payment-app account, and the tool to use.\n2. List filing and payment deadlines that may be affected (income tax, sales tax, payroll, company filings), each marked \"confirm locally\", with any already missed at the top.\n3. Estimate the size of the job: months times accounts times rough transactions a month, and a realistic number of sessions.\n4. Set the order of work: periods tied to the nearest deadline first, then oldest to newest.\n\nSections: Deadlines, Accounts in scope, Size of the job, Order of work, Open questions. Stop for approval."},{"id":"gather","file":"steps/02-gather.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"books-catch-up/02-gather.md","text":"# Step 2: Gather records\n\n1. A checklist of what to download or collect per account and month: statements, payment-app and marketplace reports, sales invoices, supplier bills, payroll reports, loan statements, tax letters.\n2. Where to find missing items: email searches for receipts, supplier portals, duplicate invoices on request, bank statement copies.\n3. A simple folder and file-naming scheme by month.\n4. A gap log to fill as they go: what is missing, why, and the substitute evidence (statement line plus a written note).\n\nSections: Collection checklist, Where to find missing items, Filing scheme, Gap log template. Stop for approval."},{"id":"code","file":"steps/03-code.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"books-catch-up/03-code.md","text":"# Step 3: Code transactions\n\n1. Agree a short category list (or map to the existing chart of accounts) and coding rules for recurring payees, so the same thing is coded the same way every month.\n2. Code the month the person provides in a table: date, description, amount, category, tax treatment to confirm, evidence held or missing, confidence.\n3. Separate transfers between own accounts, owner money in and out, loan receipts and repayments (principal is not an expense), and payment-app payouts (gross sales and fees, not the net deposit).\n4. Put anything unclear in a questions list rather than guessing.\n\nSections: Coding rules, Coded month, Special items, Questions. Repeat per month as the person supplies data. Stop for approval."},{"id":"reconcile","file":"steps/04-reconcile.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","artifact":"books-catch-up/04-reconcile.md","text":"# Step 4: Reconcile\n\n1. For each account and month, reconcile book balance to statement balance; opening balance must equal the prior month's closing figure.\n2. Explain each difference (timing, duplicate, missing entry, mis-keyed amount) and propose the correction; never plug.\n3. Check unpaid invoices and bills lists against later payments.\n4. Summarise status per month: reconciled, or open with the amount and the next check.\n\nSections: Reconciliation status, Differences and corrections, Open receivables and payables, Still open. Stop for approval."},{"id":"handover","file":"steps/05-handover.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","artifact":"books-catch-up/05-handover.md","text":"# Step 5: Handover and keep current\n\n1. Build the accountant pack: period covered, accounts reconciled, profit summary per month, the gap log, the questions list, and items needing judgement (mixed-use costs, large purchases that may be assets, owner transactions).\n2. Draft a short covering note to the accountant.\n3. Set a routine so it does not happen again: a weekly 20-minute coding session, a monthly reconciliation date, and receipts captured on the day.\n\nSections: Accountant pack, Covering note, Routine from now on."}]}
